e most vulnerable  with a third booster  as well as of course we're already doing  the immunosuppressed uh with as part of  their primary  vaccine treatment  sorry minister the reason i push on this  is at times this week it does feel like  the messaging from government has been  in different places at the weekend you  know that's an end no vaccine passports  the following day we find out that they  remain on the table telling the prime  minister incredibly optimistic but at  the same time suggesting there are  circumstances in which as you've just  conceded we could go back into lockdown  i mean it's a little bit early in the  year to be asking these kind of  questions i will concede but you can't  for example guarantee right now that  we'll all be able to spend christmas  with our families can you  so look the important thing to remember  is as we embark on the winter months the  virus have an inbuilt advantage whether  it be flu or the covered virus as chris  whittie set out yesterday would be uh  full heart it'd suggest that you know  this thing has already transitioned from  pandemic to endemic that is the  direction that we're taking a massive  booster campaign a really fit for  purpose surveillance variant  surveillance system a really fit for  purpose test and trace system that is  where we  think we will be able to end up we're  confident that we have the  infrastructure in place to deliver on  this but of course look we we have to  have contingency planning there is  all the time  more information coming in on how the  virus is behaving winter has a gives the  virus an inbuilt advantage boosters  reduce that advantage by you know  hopefully uh uh taking the most  vulnerable out of harm's way of the body  i i understand all that i understand all  of that minister i'm really i'm really  not looking for some kind of gotcha  moment here all i'm trying to kind of to  flesh out is the idea that we are not  out of the woods in terms of covert even  if things go as well as you expect you  know there remains the possibility  however likely or unlikely that we could  see ourselves returning to you know  public health responses the likes of  which we have seen you know in the  recent past  so again i just repeat for your viewers  um the non-pharmaceutical interventions  the lock downs were our only tool that  is no longer true that we have the  vaccines we have the test and trace  system we have the surveillance the  various surveillance system  we have the public health advice which  people followed of all ages you know  really really diligently which has  allowed us to take step four to reopen  uh our economy and  the booster campaign with flu will help  basically protect the most vulnerable  as we transition i hope this virus and i  can come back on your program and say to  you uh after winter's over hand on heart  we are the you know one of the leading  major economies of the world to  demonstrate how you transition the virus  from pandemic to endemic and then deal  with it on an annual basis through the  uh inoculation the vaccination program  we will we will all be keeping our  fingers crossed that that is the case i  i do wonder though what your advice  would be to colleagues within your party  who are openly expressing skepticism  about one key aspect of all of this the  vaccination of 12 to 15 year olds marcus  fish tweeted this sorry to have to say  it but the chief medical officer chris  whittie does not deserve the confidence  of the country in deciding to vaccinate  teenagers without good clinical reason  his words he the chief medical officer  should resign what would you say to mr  fish this morning  well all i would say is that  both the jcbi who in their guidance said  look  it is marginally more beneficial  to be uh vaccinated the 12 healthy 12 15  year olds it's well worth reminding uh  your audience that we've been  vaccinating 12th teen year olds who are  more vulnerable uh as per the jcbi  advice the jcbi 